\section{Example of General Action Integral}

\subsection{General Definitions}

Consider the total action, which is a function of \emph{continuous} paths
that have in general variable end points (possibly constrained to have
values in a given manifold) that take variable lengths of time to propagate
from an initial point to a final point, defined by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}\left( x\left[ \tau ,a,b\right] \right) =\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau
,a,b\right) =\int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\mathcal{L}\left( x,%
\dot{x},t\right) dt
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{align}
\left\vert x\left( 0\right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert a\right\rangle \in 
\mathcal{M}_{i}  \notag \\
\left\vert x\left( \tau \right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert b\right\rangle
\in \mathcal{M}_{f}
\end{align}%
We denote the explicit dependence of the path on its end points and time
duration as $x\left[ \tau ,a,b\right] ,$ which in general is a set of paths
that we also name as $\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,a,b\right) \subseteq \mathcal{P%
}\left( \tau ,\mathcal{M}_{i},\mathcal{M}_{f}\right) \subseteq $ $\mathcal{P}%
\left( \Upsilon ,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{H}\right) $, when this dependence is
implicit we just denote the path as $x$. The set of time intervals $\Upsilon 
$ is defined as 
\begin{equation}
\Upsilon =\left\{ \left[ 0,\tau \right] |0\leq \tau \leq \infty \right\}
\end{equation}%
Note that $x^{-1}\left( b\right) $ is well defined as the time taken for the
path $x\left( t\right) $ \emph{to first} reach the end point $\left\vert
b\right\rangle $. However $x^{-1}\left( t\right) $ for $t<\tau $ might not
be well defined, e.g. the same point $\left\vert c\right\rangle $ might be
visited many times during the time evolution.

The dependence of the action on the time duration $\tau ,$ and the end
points of the paths can be described by considering different paths
belonging to $\mathcal{P}\left( \Upsilon ,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{H}\right) $.
The first order variation of the total action with respect to path and time
duration change is 
\begin{equation}
\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) =\int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left(
b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\dot{x},\tau \right) }{%
\delta x\left( t\right) }\delta x\left( t\right) \right\} dt+\mathcal{L}%
\left( x,\dot{x},\tau \right) \delta \tau
\end{equation}%
the first term gives changes in the action due to path change, the second
due to path non dependent changes when the duration changes. These latter
changes come from the explicit dependence of the action on time. When the
duration changes and the end points remain fixed there has to be a change of
path, so the first term can also describes the effect of duration changes
e.g. for every $\delta \tau $ change there is an associated path change $%
\delta x_{\tau }\left( t\right) $. Noting that 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }%
=\int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,%
\dot{x},t^{\prime }\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }dt^{\prime }=\frac{%
\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }
\end{equation}%
we obtain the first order variation of the action in terms of the Lagrangian
as 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) =\int_{t=0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) dt= 
\notag \\
&&\left. {}\right. \qquad \int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ 
\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }%
\delta x\left( t\right) +\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{%
\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) }\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) \right\} dt+%
\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},\tau \right) \delta \tau
\end{eqnarray}%
which after using integration by parts gives 
\begin{align}
\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) & =\int_{t=0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x}%
,t\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }-\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ \frac{\delta 
\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) }%
\right\} \right\} \delta x\left( t\right) dt  \notag \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad +\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},\tau \right)
\delta \tau +\left. \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \dot{x}\left(
t\right) }\delta x\left( t\right) \right\vert _{t=0}^{t=\tau =x^{-1}\left(
b\right) }
\end{align}%
The paths can vary in three independent ways that generate all possible
changes:

\begin{enumerate}
\item their end points and durations are fixed, but their duration is fixed

\item their end points can vary within in a subset of $\mathcal{H}$

\item their duration is not fixed, but their end points are.
\end{enumerate}

The incremental functions $\delta x\left( t\right) $ are thus of three
corresponding types:

\begin{description}
\item[$\bullet $] the end points of the incremental paths are zero; $\delta
x\left( t\right) \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,0,0\right) $

\item[$\bullet $] the end points of the incremental paths lie in $\mathcal{M}%
_{i},\mathcal{M}_{f}\subseteq \mathcal{H}$; these transform paths to paths
of different end points but keep the time duration fixed, so that $\delta
x\left( t\right) \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\mathcal{M}_{i},\mathcal{M}%
_{f}\right) $.

\item We note that in this case one only needs to consider paths of the form 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \delta x\left( t\right) \right\rangle & \in \mathcal{P}\left(
\tau ,\mathcal{M}_{i},\mathcal{M}_{f}\right)  \notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( t\right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert \varphi
_{ab}\left( t\right) \right\rangle \quad t\in \left( 0,\tau \right)  \notag
\\
\left\vert \delta x\left( 0\right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert \delta
a\right\rangle  \notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( \tau \right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert \delta
b\right\rangle
\end{align}%
where $\left\vert \varphi _{ab}\left( t\right) \right\rangle $ is a fixed
continuous path connecting $\left\{ \left\vert \delta a\right\rangle
,\left\vert \delta b\right\rangle \right\} $. All other paths belonging to $%
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\mathcal{M}_{i},\mathcal{M}_{f}\right) $ are
generated by adding increments belonging to $\mathcal{P}\left( \tau
,0,0\right) $

\item[$\bullet $] the incremental paths transforming 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle
\right) \rightarrow \mathcal{P}\left( \tau +\delta \tau ,\left\vert
a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle \right)
\end{equation}%
are defined by 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \delta x\left( 0\right) \right\rangle & =0  \notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( t\right) \right\rangle & =-\left\vert \dot{x}%
\left( t\right) \right\rangle \delta \tau ;\qquad t\in \left( 0,\tau \right]
\notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( \tau +\delta \tau \right) \right\rangle &
=\left\vert b\right\rangle
\end{align}%
where $\left\vert x\left( t\right) \right\rangle $ is a reference path
belonging to $\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert
b\right\rangle \right) $. These change paths keeping the end points fixed
but allowing the time duration between end points to vary. We note that one
needs only one increment of this type to transform from $\mathcal{P}\left(
\tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle \right) $ to $%
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau +\delta \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert
b\right\rangle \right) $ all other paths are then generated by increments of 
$\in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,0,0\right) $.

\item If we start with $\left\vert x\left( t\right) \right\rangle \in 
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle
\right) $ then we can define the increment as above and obtain $\left\vert
x_{1}\left( t\right) \right\rangle \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau +\delta \tau
,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle \right) $, then we can
define a new increment 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \delta x\left( 0\right) \right\rangle & =0  \notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( t\right) \right\rangle & =-\left\vert \dot{x}%
_{1}\left( t\right) \right\rangle \delta \tau ;\qquad t\in \left( 0,\tau
+\delta \tau \right]  \notag \\
\left\vert \delta x\left( \tau +2\delta \tau \right) \right\rangle &
=\left\vert b\right\rangle
\end{align}%
and proceed until we get to $\tau +\Delta \tau $, i.e. we continue (deform)
the path in a sort of analytic fashion.
\end{description}

All increment functions can be decomposed into a sum of these three types so
that a general first order change is of the form 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \delta ^{\left( 1\right) }x\left( t\right) \right\rangle
=\left\vert \delta _{F}x\left( t\right) \right\rangle +\left\vert \delta
_{E}x\left( t\right) \right\rangle +\left\vert \delta _{\tau }x\left(
t\right) \right\rangle
\end{equation}%
where the individual increments can be taken as independent.

The paths are invariably determined by parameters that characterize the
boundary conditions that they must fulfill. These parameters then become the
variables of the problem, i.e.%
\begin{equation}
x\left( t\right) =x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right)
\end{equation}%
and we have extended the notation so that 
\begin{equation}
x:\mathfrak{a}\times \Upsilon \rightarrow \mathcal{H}
\end{equation}%
and $\mathfrak{a}$ is a linear space for unconstrained paths or a manifold, $%
\mathfrak{N}\subseteq \mathfrak{a}$, when the paths must satisfy some
boundary conditions. Some examples are:%
\begin{equation}
x\left( \mathbf{\alpha },t\right) =\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }\alpha
_{j}t^{j};\quad \mathbf{\alpha \in }\mathbb{R}^{\infty }
\end{equation}

or%
\begin{equation}
x\left( \mathbf{z,}t\right) =\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }\nu _{j}e^{i\left(
\kappa _{j}+\omega _{j}t\right) }=\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }z_{j}e^{i\omega
_{j}t};\quad z_{j}=\nu _{j}e^{i\kappa _{j}};\quad \mathbf{z\in }\mathbb{C}%
^{\infty }
\end{equation}

or%
\begin{equation}
x\left( \mathbf{z},t\right) =\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }\mathbf{z}\left(
t\right) e^{i\omega _{j}t}\left\vert \varphi _{j}\right\rangle ;\quad 
\mathbf{z}\left( t\right) =\nu _{j}\left( t\right) e^{i\kappa _{j}\left(
t\right) }\mathbf{\in }\mathbb{C}^{\infty }
\end{equation}%
where $\left\{ \left\vert \varphi _{j}\right\rangle \right\} $ is a basis
for $\mathcal{H}$. The action can be expressed in terms of these parameters
as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}\left( x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) ,\tau
,a,b\right) =\int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\mathcal{L}\left(
x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) ,\dot{x}\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right)
t\right) dt=\int_{t=0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\mathcal{L}\left( 
\mathbf{\alpha },\mathbf{\dot{\alpha},}t\right) dt
\end{equation}

The variation of the action as the paths change can be expressed in terms of
derivatives wrt these parameters, which can be developed as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{A}\left( x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) ,\tau
,a,b\right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}} &=&\int_{0}^{t=\tau =x^{-1}\left(
b\right) }\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right)
,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{%
\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}}dt  \notag \\
&=&\int_{t=0}^{t=\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{L%
}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }\frac{\partial x\left( 
\mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}}+\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}%
\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) }\frac{\partial 
\dot{x}\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}}\right\} dt
\end{eqnarray}%
The incremental path change expressed in terms of these parameters is 
\begin{equation}
\delta x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) =\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }\frac{%
\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}}\delta
\alpha _{j}
\end{equation}%
which in particular gives for $t=\tau $ 
\begin{equation}
\delta x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) =\sum_{0\leq j\leq \infty }%
\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) }{\partial \alpha _{j}}%
\delta \alpha _{j}=\delta b
\end{equation}%
that along with 
\begin{equation}
x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) =b
\end{equation}%
constrains the possible values of $\delta \mathbf{\alpha }$ and $\mathbf{%
\alpha }$.

The parameter manifold of paths with end points $\left\{ a,b\right\} $ and
time duration $\tau $ is denoted by $\mathfrak{N}\left( \tau ,a,b\right) $,
constructively we can describe this manifold as: for each $\tau $ find the
set $\mathbf{\alpha }\left( \tau \right) \equiv \left\{ \alpha \right\} $
such that $x\left( \alpha ,\tau \right) =b$ and $x\left( \alpha ,0\right) =a$%
.

\subsection{Example}

We are interested in the case where the initial point, $\left\vert
a\right\rangle $, is fixed. We define the Lagrangian to be 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) =2x\left( t\right) +4\dot{x}\left(
t\right) +6t^{2}
\end{equation}%
(which has an explicit time dependence in the term $6t^{2}$, if the
coefficients of $x\left( t\right) $ or $\dot{x}\left( t\right) $ were time
dependent this would also introduce an explicit time dependence), where%
\begin{align}
x\left( t\right) & =\alpha _{0}+\alpha _{1}t+\alpha _{2}t^{2}+\alpha
_{3}\sin \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }  \notag \\
\dot{x}\left( t\right) & =\alpha _{1}+2\alpha _{2}t+\frac{2\pi \alpha _{3}}{%
\tau }\cos \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }
\end{align}%
so that 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) & =2\left( \alpha _{0}+\alpha
_{1}t+\alpha _{2}t^{2}+\alpha _{3}\sin \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }\right)  \notag \\
& +4\left( \alpha _{1}+2\alpha _{2}t+\frac{2\pi \alpha _{3}}{\tau }\cos 
\frac{2\pi t}{\tau }\right) +6t^{2}
\end{align}

The value of the action $\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
& \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) =  \notag \\
& \qquad \left. \underset{}{}\right\vert _{0}^{\tau }\left( 2\alpha
_{0}t+\left( \alpha _{1}+4\alpha _{2}\right) t^{2}+\left( \frac{2}{3}\alpha
_{2}+2\right) t^{3}\right) +4\alpha _{3}\sin \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }-\frac{%
\alpha _{3}\tau }{\pi }\cos \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }  \notag \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad =2\alpha _{0}\tau
+\left( \alpha _{1}+4\alpha _{2}\right) \tau ^{2}+\left( \frac{2}{3}\alpha
_{2}+2\right) \tau ^{3}
\end{align}%
and thus%
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\partial \tau } &=&%
\frac{\partial \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\partial t} \\
&=&2\alpha _{0}+2\left( \alpha _{1}+4\alpha _{2}\right) \tau +3\left( \frac{2%
}{3}\alpha _{2}+2\right) \tau ^{2}=\mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},\tau \right)
\end{eqnarray}%
which only defines the Lagrangian up to terms that do not change the value
of the action. These would be terms that are total time derivatives of
periodic functions of period $\tau $. In this case the term involving $%
\alpha _{3}$ is such a cyclic variable (i.e. the value of the action is the
same for this variable at the beginning of the time duration as the end). $%
\lceil $The increment functions that leave the end points fixed for a fixed
time duration $\tau $, i.e. belong to $\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,0,0\right) $,
are given by 
\begin{equation}
g\left( \tau ,t\right) =F\left( \sin \frac{2\pi t}{\tau }\right)
\end{equation}%
for arbitrary continuous functions $g$.$\rfloor $

The paths are parameterized by $\left\{ \alpha _{0},\alpha _{1},\alpha
_{2},\alpha _{3},\tau \right\} $ i.e. $x\left[ \alpha _{0},\alpha
_{1},\alpha _{2},\alpha _{3},\tau \right] $ is a family of paths. If the end
points are completely unconstrained then there are no relationships between
these parameters/variables and $\mathbf{\alpha \in }\mathbb{R}^{4},\tau \in %
\left[ 0,\infty \right] $.

The values of the paths and their velocities at the boundaries are 
\begin{align}
x\left( \tau \right) & =\alpha _{0}+\alpha _{1}\tau +\alpha _{2}\tau ^{2} 
\notag \\
x\left( 0\right) & =\alpha _{0}  \notag \\
\dot{x}\left( \tau \right) & =\alpha _{1}+2\alpha _{2}\tau +\frac{2\pi
\alpha _{3}}{\tau }  \notag \\
\dot{x}\left( 0\right) & =\alpha _{1}+\frac{2\pi \alpha _{3}}{\tau }
\end{align}%
The path parameters $\left\{ \alpha _{0},\alpha _{1},\alpha _{2}\right\} $
are determined by the end point conditions (the value of $\alpha _{3}$ can
be arbitrary). Thus if one sets end points 
\begin{align}
x\left( 0\right) & =a  \notag \\
x\left( \tau \right) & =b
\end{align}%
for a given $\tau $ one must constrain $\left\{ \alpha _{0},\alpha
_{1},\alpha _{2}\right\} $, the variables that with $\alpha _{3}$
characterize the paths, by 
\begin{align}
\alpha _{0}& =a  \notag \\
\alpha _{0}+\alpha _{1}\tau +\alpha _{2}\tau ^{2}& =b
\end{align}%
so that the end point value 
\begin{equation}
x\left( \tau \right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2} \\ 
a%
\end{array}%
\right) =b
\end{equation}%
which implies that 
\begin{align}
& \alpha _{1}\tau +\alpha _{2}\tau ^{2}=b-a  \notag \\
& \equiv \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) =b-a  \notag \\
& \equiv F_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =b-a  \notag \\
\mathbf{\alpha }& =\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right)  \notag \\
& \equiv \mathbf{\alpha }\in F_{\tau }^{-1}\left[ b-a\right]
\end{align}%
where $F_{\tau }$ is a linear function 
\begin{equation}
F_{\tau }:\mathfrak{a}\rightarrow \mathcal{H}
\end{equation}%
so that $F\left( \mathbf{\alpha },\tau \right) =\left\vert b\right\rangle
\in \mathcal{H}$. The plane of parameters, $\mathfrak{a}=\left\{ \alpha
_{1},\alpha _{2}\right\} $ are generated by the unit vectors that make the
angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{b-a}{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $ with
the vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $. We can then \emph{choose a} path $\mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left(
\tau ,a,b\right) $ from the set of paths $\mathbf{\alpha }\left[ \tau ,a,b%
\right] $ (all with end points $\left\{ a,b\right\} $) that form the
preimage of $F_{\tau }$ and generate all other paths with the same end
points from it.

The path parameters $\left\{ \alpha _{1},\alpha _{2}\right\} $ lie in a
plane given by 
\begin{equation}
\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2} \\ 
b-a%
\end{array}%
\right) =0
\end{equation}%
and all increments that are $\bot $ to $\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) $ produce variations that give allowed $\alpha ^{\prime }s$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) =0
\end{equation}%
which implies that 
\begin{equation}
\delta \mathbf{\alpha \,}\bot \,\left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\tau \\ 
\tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \equiv F_{\tau }\left( \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =0
\end{equation}%
So that $\left\{ \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right\} $ is a plane perpendicular
to $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $. Note that if $\tau $ is held fixed the increment condition is the
same \emph{for any} $a$ or $b$. Thus 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{\alpha }=\mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\mathbf{+}\delta \mathbf{\alpha
;\quad }F_{\tau }\left( \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =0;\quad F_{\tau
}\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\right) =b-a
\end{equation}%
will generate all paths with fixed end points $\left\{ a,b\right\} $ and
duration $\tau $. The variables of the fixed end point and fixed duration
problem can be either $\delta \mathbf{\alpha \,}\bot \,\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $ incrementing form a fixed unit vector $\mathbf{\alpha }%
_{ref}\left( \tau ,a,b\right) $ that makes an angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{%
b-a}{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $ with the vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $ or vary over all vectors $\alpha \mathbf{\hat{\alpha}}\left( \tau
,a,b\right) $, where $\mathbf{\hat{\alpha}}\left( \tau ,a,b\right) $ makes
the angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{b-a}{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $
with the vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $.

The increment paths defined by 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{\tilde{\alpha}}=\mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\mathbf{+}\delta \mathbf{%
\alpha ;\quad }F_{\tau }\left( \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =\delta
b;\quad F_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\right) =b-a
\end{equation}%
will generate all paths with end points $\left\{ a,b+\delta b\right\} $ and
duration $\tau $. These increment paths form a plane that is at an angle $%
cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{\delta b}{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $ to the
vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $. All of these paths can be obtained by choosing a reference path $%
\left\vert x_{ref}\left( t\right) \right\rangle \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau
,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle \right) $ then
transforming it to $\left\vert y_{ref}\left( t\right) \right\rangle
=\left\vert x_{ref}\left( t\right) +\delta x_{0}\left( t\right)
\right\rangle \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle
,\left\vert b\right\rangle +\left\vert \delta b\right\rangle \right) $, by
some fixed increment path whose parameters satisfy $F_{\tau }\left( \delta 
\mathbf{\alpha }_{0}\right) =\delta b$, then transform $\left\vert
y_{ref}\left( t\right) \right\rangle $ by increment paths that belong to $%
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,0,0\right) $ thus generating all of $\mathcal{P}%
\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert b\right\rangle
+\left\vert \delta b\right\rangle \right) $. Thus end point variation can be
achieved by starting from a fixed unit vector $\mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left(
\tau ,a,b\right) $ that makes an angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{b-a}{\tau 
\sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $ with the vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $ and incrementing by multiples of a variable vector that makes an
angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{\delta b}{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $%
with $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) .$

The equation characterizing paths with fixed end points $\left\{ a,b\right\} 
$ and time duration $\tau +\delta \tau $ is 
\begin{align}
& \left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau +\delta \tau & \left( \tau +\delta \tau \right) ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1}+\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}+\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
b-a%
\end{array}%
\right) =0  \notag \\
& =\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1}+\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}+\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
b-a%
\end{array}%
\right) +\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\delta \tau & 2\tau \delta \tau & 0%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1}+\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}+\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
b-a%
\end{array}%
\right)  \notag \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad +\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
0 & \delta \tau ^{2} & 0%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1}+\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}+\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
b-a%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{align}

Thus the first order change satisfies 
\begin{align}
& \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 2\tau%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \delta \tau +\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) =0  \notag \\
& \Leftrightarrow \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 2\tau%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \delta \tau =-\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right)  \notag \\
& \Leftrightarrow \frac{d}{d\tau }\left\{ \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\} \delta \tau =-\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right)  \notag \\
& \Leftrightarrow F_{\tau }\left( \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =-\frac{%
dF_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) }{d\tau }\delta \tau \text{ so that 
}\left\{ \delta \mathbf{\alpha }\right\} =F_{\tau }^{-1}\left[ -\frac{%
dF_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) }{d\tau }\delta \tau \right]
\end{align}%
where $F_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) =b-a$. One can see that 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{d\tau }\left\{ \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) \right\} =\frac{dx\left( \mathbf{\alpha },\tau \right) }{d\tau }=%
\dot{x}\left( \mathbf{\alpha },\tau \right)
\end{equation}
and also that the increment vectors $\delta \mathbf{\alpha }$ form a plane
that is at an angle $cos^{-1}\left\{ \frac{-\frac{dF_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{%
\alpha }\right) }{d\tau }\delta \tau }{\tau \sqrt{1+\tau ^{2}}}\right\} $ to
the vector $\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\tau & \tau ^{2}%
\end{array}%
\right) $.

Again we can choose a reference path $\left\vert x_{ref}\left( t\right)
\right\rangle \in \mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle
,\left\vert b\right\rangle \right) $ then transform it to $\left\vert
y_{ref}\left( t\right) \right\rangle =$ $\left\vert x_{ref}\left( t\right)
\right\rangle +\left\vert \delta x_{0}\left( t\right) \right\rangle \in 
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau +\delta \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert
b\right\rangle \right) $, by some fixed increment path whose parameters
satisfy $F_{\tau }\left( \delta \mathbf{\alpha }_{0}\right) =-\frac{dF_{\tau
}\left( \mathbf{\alpha }\right) }{d\tau }\delta \tau $, then transform $%
\left\vert y_{ref}\left( t\right) \right\rangle $ by increment paths that
belong to $\mathcal{P}\left( \tau ,0,0\right) $ thus generating all of $%
\mathcal{P}\left( \tau +\delta \tau ,\left\vert a\right\rangle ,\left\vert
b\right\rangle \right) $. The increment path is 
\begin{equation}
\delta x_{0}\left( t\right) =-\dot{x}_{ref}\left( \mathbf{\alpha },t\right)
\delta \tau
\end{equation}

The connection between the paths and the path parameters is explicitly%
\begin{equation}
x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
t & t^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\alpha _{1} \\ 
\alpha _{2} \\ 
a%
\end{array}%
\right)
\end{equation}%
leading to 
\begin{equation}
\delta x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
t & t^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) =\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha
_{1}}\delta \alpha _{1}+\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{%
\partial \alpha _{2}}\delta \alpha _{2}
\end{equation}%
so that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{1}} &=&t
\notag \\
\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{2}}
&=&t^{2}
\end{eqnarray}%
hence at $t=\tau $ 
\begin{equation}
\delta x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) =\delta b=\left( 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\tau & \tau ^{2} & 1%
\end{array}%
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{c}
\delta \alpha _{1} \\ 
\delta \alpha _{2} \\ 
0%
\end{array}%
\right) =\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) }{\partial
\alpha _{1}}\delta \alpha _{1}+\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau
\right) }{\partial \alpha _{2}}\delta \alpha _{2}
\end{equation}%
which gives the general formulation of how $\delta \mathbf{\alpha }$ is
characterized by the end point condition at a fixed time duration. For the
duration variation we have after we have chosen a set of reference, $\mathbf{%
\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) $, paths for each $\tau ,$ (so that $%
y_{ref}\left( t\right) =x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right)
,t\right) $%
\begin{eqnarray}
\delta x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) ,t\right) &=&-\frac{%
dx\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) ,t\right) }{d\tau }\delta
\tau =\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right)
,t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{1}}\delta \alpha _{1}+\frac{\partial x\left( 
\mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) ,t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{2}}%
\delta \alpha _{2} \\
&\equiv &-\frac{dx\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) ,t\right) 
}{d\tau }=\frac{\partial x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right)
,t\right) }{\partial \alpha _{1}}\frac{d\alpha _{1}}{dt}+\frac{\partial
x\left( \mathbf{\alpha }_{ref}\left( \tau \right) ,t\right) }{\partial
\alpha _{2}}\frac{d\alpha _{2}}{dt}
\end{eqnarray}%
Hence for our example 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{eqnarray}
\delta x_{F}\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) &=&\tau \delta \alpha
_{1}+\tau ^{2}\delta \alpha _{2}=0 \\
\delta x_{b}\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) &=&\tau \delta \alpha
_{1}+\tau ^{2}\delta \alpha _{2}=\delta b \\
\delta x_{\tau }\left( \mathbf{\alpha ,}\tau \right) &=&\tau \delta \alpha
_{1}+\tau ^{2}\delta \alpha _{2}=-\left( \alpha _{1}+2\tau \alpha
_{2}\right) \delta \tau
\end{eqnarray}

The total first order variation of the action, when one holds the initial
point of the path fixed, is 
\end{subequations}
\begin{align}
\delta ^{\left( 1\right) }\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) & =\delta _{F}%
\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) +\delta _{\tau _{E}}\mathcal{A}\left(
x,\tau ,a,b\right)   \notag \\
& +\delta _{E}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) +\delta _{\tau _{P}}%
\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) 
\end{align}%
we can develop each term as 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{eqnarray}
\delta _{F}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right)  &=&\int_{0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{\dot{A}}\left( x,\tau
,a,b\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }\delta x_{F}\left( t\right) \right\}
dt \\
\delta _{\tau _{F}}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right)  &=&\left(
\int_{0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}\left( x,%
\dot{x},t\right) }{\partial t}dt\right) \delta \tau =\mathcal{L}\left( x,%
\dot{x},\tau \right) \delta \tau  \\
\delta _{E}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right)  &=&\int_{0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{\dot{A}}\left( x,\tau
,a,b\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }\delta x_{b}\left( t\right) \right\}
dt+\left. \frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta \dot{x}%
\left( t\right) }\delta x_{b}\left( t\right) \right\vert _{0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) } \\
\delta _{\tau _{P}}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right)  &=&\int_{0}^{\tau
=x^{-1}\left( b\right) }\left\{ \frac{\delta \mathcal{\dot{A}}\left( x,\tau
,a,b\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }\delta x_{\tau }\left( t\right)
\right\} dt+\left. \frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{%
\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) }\delta x_{\tau }\left( t\right) \right\vert
_{0}^{\tau =x^{-1}\left( b\right) }
\end{eqnarray}%
where 
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{\dot{A}}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) =\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,%
\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta x\left( t\right) }-\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ \frac{%
\delta \mathcal{L}\left( x,\dot{x},t\right) }{\delta \dot{x}\left( t\right) }%
\right\} =2-0=2
\end{equation}%
Note that $\delta _{F}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) $ means the first
order variation of the action wrt paths when $\tau $, $a$ and $b$ are
constant, $\delta _{\tau _{E}}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) +$ $%
\delta _{\tau _{P}}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) $ means the first
order variation of the action wrt time duration when $a$ and $b$ are
constant and $\delta _{E}\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) $ means the
first order variation of the action wrt the end point $b$ when $x$, $a$ and $%
\tau $ are constant. Thus the first order variation is zero when the
following conditions hold simultaneously 
\begin{subequations}
\label{cond}
\begin{align}
\frac{\delta \mathcal{\dot{A}}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\partial x\left(
t\right) }& =0\qquad \forall t\in \left[ 0,\tau \right]   \label{cond1} \\
\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\partial t}& =0 \\
\frac{\partial \mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau ,a,b\right) }{\partial b}& =0
\end{align}%
Note the in eq. $\left( \ref{cond1}\right) $ the paths have fixed end points
and the duration is fixed.

We now reexpress the action 
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau,a,b\right) =2\alpha_{0}\tau+\left( \alpha
_{1}+4\alpha_{2}\right) \tau^{2}+\left( \frac{2}{3}\alpha_{2}+2\right)
\tau^{3}
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau,a,b\right) & =2a\tau+\left(
\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\tau\right) \tau+\frac{11}{3}\alpha_{2}\tau^{2}+2\tau
^{3}  \notag \\
& =2a\tau+b-a+\frac{11}{3}\alpha_{2}\tau^{2}+2\tau^{3}
\end{align}
which explicitly expresses the action in terms of the end point values, time
duration and paths. Hence 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau,a,b\right) }{\partial b}=1-\frac {11}{%
3}\frac{\partial\alpha_{2}}{\partial b}\tau^{2}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{A}\left( x,\tau,a,b\right) }{\partial t}=2a+\frac {22%
}{3}\alpha_{2}\tau+6\tau^{2}
\end{equation}

\end{document}
